urrent chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \nYou hold 4♦ 2♣, and the board shows: 9♦ A♦ J♥ K♦. This gives you four cards to a flush (A, K, 9, 4 of diamonds), needing one more diamond to complete it. There are nine diamonds in a suit, and five are already accounted for (A♦, K♦, 9♦, 4♦, and possibly another), but only four diamonds are visible — your two hole cards include the 4♦, so three diamonds are on the board (A♦, K♦, 9♦). That means there are six unseen diamonds left in the deck (13 total - 3 on board - 1 in your hand = 9 remaining unknowns, minus non-diamonds). With only one card to come (the river), you’re drawing to a flush, which gives you about 9 outs (standard flush draw) — wait: you have four diamonds in hand/board combined (your 4♦ + A♦, K♦, 9♦), so yes, you have a **four-card flush draw**, meaning 9 outs remain (since 13 - 4 = 9 diamonds left unseen; but not all may be available).\n\nActually, correct count: There are 13 diamonds total. On board: A♦, K♦, 9♦. In your hand: 4♦. So that’s 4 diamonds seen. Therefore, 9 diamonds remain unaccounted for — but some may have been folded or are in opponent’s hand. Still, assuming randomness, you have **9 outs** to hit a diamond on the river for a flush.\n\nAdditionally, with A♦ and K♦ on board, any diamond could also pair with high cards — but your own high card is only a 4, so even if you make the flush, if opponent also has a diamond, they might have a higher one (e.g., Q♦, 10♦, etc.).\n\nHowever, your current hand strength is very weak: just Ace-high with no pair, no straight draw (you’d need 10-Q for a straight, but you have 4-2, so no). Only hope is the flush.\n\nOpponent has checked through all rounds so far (action: 0 on flop, turn). That suggests either:\n- They are trapping with a strong hand (like top pair, two pair, or better),\n- Or they missed and are giving up,\n- Or they are on a draw too and checking to control pot.\n\nBut since the board is coordinated (three broadway cards and